{"step_index":177,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:00:27Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:00:27+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:00:27+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py`\nTotal Lines: 348\nTotal Bytes: 15551\nShowing lines 120 to 250\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n120: \n121: _FORBIDDEN_CMD = re.compile(\n122:     r\"(?m)^\\s*(?:(?:private|protected|noncomputable|local|scoped)\\s+)*\"\n123:     r\"(namespace|section|variable|universe|notation\\w*|infixl?|infixr|prefix|postfix|\"\n124:     r\"macro\\w*|syntax\\w*|declare_syntax_cat|elab\\w*|initialize|builtin_initialize|\"\n125:     r\"run_cmd|run_elab|run_meta)\\b\")\n126: \n127: # Lean elaboration can run arbitrary code: `#eval IO.Process.run …`, `run_cmd`,\n128: # a user `elab`, an `@[init]` hook. Agents are only allowed to run the checker,\n129: # and the verifier compiles their files with full user privileges, so anything\n130: # that reaches IO or the metaprogramming API is refused *before* Lean sees the\n131: # file — that refusal is what makes \"only check.sh may run\" an actual boundary.\n132: _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN = re.compile(\n133:     r\"\\b(native_decide|ofReduceBool|trustCompiler|skipKernelTC|implemented_by|\"\n134:     r\"extern|axiom|unsafe\\w*|run_tac|run_cmd|run_elab|run_meta|addDecl\\w*|\"\n135:     r\"Lean|\\w*IO|System|Process|MetaM|CoreM|TermElabM|CommandElabM|TacticM|\"\n136:     r\"macro\\w*|elab\\w*|syntax\\w*|notation\\w*|initialize|builtin_initialize)\\b\"\n137:     r\"|#eval|#exit|\\bdebug\\.|@\\[\\s*(?:init|builtin_init|command_elab|term_elab|\"\n138:     r\"tactic|extern|implemented_by)\")\n139: \n140: _ALLOWED_IMPORTS = {\"Mathlib\", \"LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable\",\n141:                     \"LeanProject.GraphInvariants\"}\n142: \n143: \n144: def _norm(text: str) -> str:\n145:     return \"\\n\".join(line.rstrip() for line in text.replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").split(\"\\n\"))\n146: \n147: \n148: def _strip_comments(src: str) -> str:\n149:     src = re.sub(r\"/-.*?-/\", \" \", src, flags=re.S)\n150:     return re.sub(r\"--[^\\n]*\", \" \", src)\n151: \n152: \n153: def _prefix(original: str):\n154:     \"\"\"Everything up to the first theorem: imports, options, `Candidate`.\"\"\"\n155:     i = original.find(\"abbrev Candidate\")\n156:     j = original.find(\"\\n\\ntheorem \", i) if i >= 0 else -1\n157:     return original[:j] if j >= 0 else None\n158: \n159: \n160: def structural_check(original: str, returned: str):\n161:     \"\"\"(ok, reason): may the returned file be compiled as an answer at all?\"\"\"\n162:     orig, ret = _norm(original), _norm(returned)\n163:     pre = _prefix(orig)\n164:     if pre is None:\n165:         return False, \"original challenge is malformed\"\n166:     if not ret.startswith(pre):\n167:         return False, \"statement changed: text above the first theorem differs\"\n168:     rest = ret[len(pre):]\n169:     if rest and not rest.startswith(\"\\n\"):\n170:         return False, \"statement changed: Candidate's last line was extended\"\n171:     code = _strip_comments(rest)\n172:     head = code.lstrip()\n173:     if head and not head.startswith(_CMD_START):\n174:         return False, \"statement changed: Candidate continues past its original end\"\n175:     m = _FORBIDDEN_CMD.search(code)\n176:     if m:\n177:         return False, f\"forbidden command `{m.group(1)}`\"\n178:     m = _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN.search(code)\n179:     if m:\n180:         return False, f\"forbidden construct `{m.group(0)}`\"\n181:     return True, \"\"\n182: \n183: \n184: def lint_text(text: str, original=None):\n185:     \"\"\"(ok, reason) for any Lean file an agent asks to elaborate.\n186: \n187:     A challenge file is held to `structural_check` against its original. Any\n188:     other file (scratch lemmas) gets the same code-execution screen plus an\n189:     import whitelist, since `import` is the other way to pull in code.\n190:     \"\"\"\n191:     if original is not None:\n192:         return structural_check(original, text)\n193:     code = _strip_comments(_norm(text))\n194:     for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^\\s*import\\s+(\\S+)\", code):\n195:         if m.group(1) not in _ALLOWED_IMPORTS:\n196:             return False, f\"import `{m.group(1)}` is not allowed\"\n197:     m = _FORBIDDEN_CMD.search(code)\n198:     if m:\n199:         return False, f\"forbidden command `{m.group(1)}`\"\n200:     m = _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN.search(code)\n201:     if m:\n202:         return False, f\"forbidden construct `{m.group(0)}`\"\n203:     return True, \"\"\n204: \n205: \n206: def solution_file(returned: str, ident: str, direction: str):\n207:     \"\"\"The returned file prepared to check one direction, or None.\n208: \n209:     The other direction's untouched `sorry` block is removed, and a declaration\n210:     is appended that forces Lean to confirm the target has exactly the type\n211:     asked for — `_root_`-qualified, so a same-named `Candidate` or target in some\n212:     other namespace cannot stand in for it.\n213:     \"\"\"\n214:     ret = _norm(returned)\n215:     other = \"refute\" if direction == \"prove\" else \"prove\"\n216:     header = {\"prove\": rf\"(?m)^theorem\\s+prove_{ident}\\s*:\\s*Candidate\\s*:=\",\n217:               \"refute\": rf\"(?m)^theorem\\s+refute_{ident}\\s*:\\s*¬\\s*Candidate\\s*:=\"}\n218:     pristine = {\"prove\": f\"theorem prove_{ident} : Candidate := by\\n  sorry\",\n219:                 \"refute\": f\"theorem refute_{ident} : ¬ Candidate := by\\n  sorry\"}\n220:     if not re.search(header[direction], _strip_comments(ret)):\n221:         return None\n222:     if pristine[direction] in ret:\n223:         return None\n224:     body = ret.replace(pristine[other], \"\")\n225:     ty = \"_root_.Candidate\" if direction == \"prove\" else \"¬ _root_.Candidate\"\n226:     return (body.rstrip() + \"\\n\\n-- verifier: the target must have exactly this type\\n\"\n227:             f\"theorem agf_verify_{direction} : {ty} := _root_.{direction}_{ident}\\n\")\n228: \n229: \n230: def do_verify(args) -> int:\n231:     from pipeline.theorem_prover import LeanSubprocessProver\n232:     from tools.prover_eval.w16 import is_timeout\n233: \n234:     index, src = _index(args.benchmark, args.partition)\n235:     truth = {}\n236:     gt = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(args.dir.rstrip(\"/\")), \".ground_truth.json\")\n237:     if os.path.exists(gt):\n238:         truth = json.load(open(gt))\n239: \n240:     CONFIG.lean_timeout_s = args.timeout\n241:     lean = LeanSubprocessProver(CONFIG)\n242:     if not lean._available:\n243:         print(\"ERROR: no Lean available — cannot verify.\")\n244:         return 2\n245: \n246:     results, counts = [], collections.Counter()\n247:     for item in index:\n248:         ident, fname = item[\"id\"], item[\"file\"]\n249:         path = os.path.join(args.dir, fname)\n250:         if not os.path.exists(path):\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":178,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:00:27Z","thinking":"**Evaluating Theorem Generation**\n\nChecking if the generated theorem matches the expected format by searching for a specific header within the output, while also verifying that the generated theorem isn't just a placeholder. Specifically, it eliminates cases where the theorem is identical to the initial \"sorry\" placeholder. The body is extracted by removing the theorem for the other direction and using it.\n\n\n**Analyzing Candidate's Proof Requirements**\n\nThe goal is to prove the `Candidate` proposition, which is a universally quantified statement about graphs. This involves demonstrating an inequality relating graph radius and roman domination number, under specific conditions. Examining the assumptions reveals a need for graph theory knowledge and potentially relevant existing library theorems.\n\n\n**Defining Graph Concepts and Inequality**\n\nNow understanding the definitions of `radius` and `romanDominationNumber`, the task focuses on proving the given inequality. The definitions clarify the relationship between graph connectivity, domination, and these computed quantities. Reconsidering the previous proof attempt, specifically for $P_6$, to check the validity of the proof.\n\n\n**Extending Observations to Path Graphs**\n\nExtending the analysis to paths ($P_{3k}$), observing that the condition $r + 1 \\le w$ holds with an increasing margin as $k$ grows, where $r$ is the radius and $w$ is the roman domination number. This is because $w$ grows linearly with $k$ while $r$ grows slower.\n\n**Investigating Graph Structure Properties**\n\nConsidering graph structures that might maximize radius while minimizing roman domination number. Exploring whether trees could exhibit this behavior, but recalling a previously proven relationship between diameter and roman domination that rules out trees as potential counterexamples.\n\n**Considering Graph with Cycles**\n\nInvestigating graphs with cycles to see if they can serve as counterexamples. Examining the specific examples of cycles, observing that the condition $r + 1 \\le w$ also holds for cycles.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"rad(G)\" \"\\gamma_R(G)\" bound","toolAction":"Searching the web","toolSummary":"Search rad(G) gamma_R(G) bound"}}]}
{"step_index":179,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:00:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:00:35+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:00:39+02:00\nThe search for \"\"rad(G)\" \"\\gamma_R(G)\" bound\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, the relationship between the **radius** of a graph, denoted as $\\text{rad}(G)$[1][2], and the **reinforcement number** of a graph—often denoted as $\\gamma_R(G)$ or variations thereof depending on the specific domination parameter being reinforced (e.g., Roman, restrained Roman, etc.)[3][4]—typically involves bounds related to the graph's vulnerability or structural constraints[5].\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n*   **$\\text{rad}(G)$ (Radius):** Defined as the minimum eccentricity among all vertices in $G$[2]. It is bounded by the diameter: $\\text{rad}(G) \\le \\text{diam}(G) \\le 2\\,\\text{rad}(G)$[6].\n*   **$\\gamma_R(G)$ (Reinforcement Number):** In general, the reinforcement number of a graph parameter is the minimum number of edges that must be added to $G$ to decrease the value of that parameter[3]. Research often focuses on \"Roman reinforcement\" or \"Restrained Roman reinforcement\" numbers[3].\n\n### Known Contexts and Bounds\nWhile there is no single universal bound connecting $\\text{rad}(G)$ and $\\gamma_R(G)$ for all graphs, they appear together in the following contexts:\n\n1.  **Vulnerability and Structure:** The reinforcement number is a vulnerability parameter[5]. Research often seeks to bound $\\gamma_R(G)$ in terms of other structural properties like the maximum degree ($\\Delta$), the order of the graph ($n$), or the radius ($\\text{rad}(G)$)[7][3].\n2.  **Specific Parameter Bounds:** \n    *   For many domination-related reinforcement numbers, the values are often small and constant for specific families (e.g., $\\gamma_R(T) = 1$ for trees of a certain order)[3].\n    *   Bounds for parameters like the 2-rainbow domination number ($\\gamma_{r2}(G)$) are frequently studied in relation to the radius[1][8].\n3.  **Radius-Forcing:** There exists a related concept called the **radius-forcing number** ($rf(G)$), which is the size of the smallest set of vertices whose distances to other vertices effectively \"force\" the radius of the graph[2]. This is a distinct parameter that measures how much of the graph is \"constrained\" by the radius.\n\nIf you are looking for a specific inequality involving a particular type of reinforcement number (e.g., Roman reinforcement vs. radius), please specify the exact domination parameter (e.g., Roman, Total, Independent), as the bounds are highly dependent on the definition of the underlying domination function[3][5].\n\nSources:\n[1] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQELmMk07M8XFzjCdXVANXM0EH2fmlREsTBdNoa_Cg4QxI7W_F_fM5hHmov53PHGxD-Pc-wuKoyJeHT5Q5E1R2rPP8fAtIkMsoSJP-9ayI0pL6habvI67ODURZQaiCuXgoAXcYMl0nb8PQABgyZaZiKp6yAxslrBGnuSuu7t2KEM7dfWXPJpnbru3F-zK0AM0qrINxMzHLtaR6iBbg==)\n[2] [uq.edu.au](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHcxp29IKJKIMwDORLMbAvy3VI2tvKggz3qKWD-fWYTix3btSQ9rag3KDBq0GleYuXOeZB1384LoVxAheYh-MagEPFi17e3GfaZsBA8LNqxMmkyvQHr5X5p0kfuYNARb_wNGHE4g7TGInweIvA=)\n[3] [urfu.ru](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG_--9H-ykFMT_bz57rkgYtQXtCigOphBbe8uNNka6Y1Tn8skP-GWrLz8JcvwO0CilppmOzFpmswJ0xdhafeM2wvtZoQA8fNtMecrGEoUaUs1yPIuuKV6Fpc0WLCXPDQIbtHbw-kn16Y6WW-T3RELGoOklTDuxsdno=)\n[4] [georgiasouthern.edu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGttDOfnnYhrZsNIuTmgrW_xgdsVd3s5ccq8mLAgDayqmDoetdNVo9-dODQlspyARrRjoLoW_LnO2Ind8ql33d3z2guVsLYJ9vv5W9kT5MV3pO4ltq1lyfomizd6KIJPtpT4AUtIXNwGqtZ94BP266cPxfxvnu0GRy8YZVd2IsGgYD9Y0-3dYbMXHvKHUA=)\n[5] [numdam.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGivKWufRK1pZALjXG753KkDS-OOg8NOh-n00fESrZOUQJLDgSxNUH9IzosigXM3PFOEfvVl_j2YXWWleAcm4VYDodcBfIvY4M2lOHVeaPLBvOT8GzCh-Y4F